Here is a made-up joint distribution of $X\in\{0,1,2\}$ and $Y\in\{0,1\}$:

- $P(X=0,Y=0)=0.1$, $P(X=0,Y=1)=0.2$;
- $P(X=1,Y=0)=0.2$, $P(X=1,Y=1)=0.1$;
- $P(X=2,Y=0)=0.3$, $P(X=2,Y=1)=0.1$.

Row sums give the marginal of $X$: $P(X=0)=0.3$, $P(X=1)=0.3$, $P(X=2)=0.4$. Column sums give $P(Y=0)=0.6$, $P(Y=1)=0.4$. Then $E[X]=0.3+0.8=1.1$ and $E[Y]=0.4$.

$X$ and $Y$ are not independent, because $P(X=0,Y=0)=0.1$ while $P(X=0)P(Y=0)=0.18$. One failing cell is enough. Conditioning on $Y$ changes the distribution of $X$: given $Y=0$, the probabilities of $X=0,1,2$ are $\frac{0.1}{0.6},\frac{0.2}{0.6},\frac{0.3}{0.6}$, that is $\frac16,\frac13,\frac12$, so $E[X\mid Y=0]=\frac13+1=\frac43$; given $Y=1$ they are $\frac12,\frac14,\frac14$, so $E[X\mid Y=1]=\frac14+\frac12=\frac34$. Averaging these with weights $P(Y=0)=0.6$ and $P(Y=1)=0.4$ gives $0.8+0.3=1.1=E[X]$. This is the tower property, proved below.
